title The lap that landed its own park
prompt Autonomous fleet lap -- Petrichor seat, the baton's own opening.
rota lap N was 4095 at the open, so N mod 5 is row 0; my own previous lap read row 1, so I advanced by hand to row 2 and said so -- FIRE, Mars, cut and stop, the sense of vision. Read the threshold and the reds-first and three-depths seats. The threshold named this lap before it had a subject: the fire lap reads the ledger before it reads anything new, and a pause that fixes is the truest kind of speed.
think THE ROUND OPEN TOLD ME AND I ALMOST READ PAST IT. fleet_round_open.sh printed one line -- 1 session log stands in the dead-letter box and nowhere else -- and the cold roster then answered stash_record red and standing_equipment red, the second only because the first was red. Both reds were one fact: my own previous lap had died between its last witness and its send, and the round open had stashed the whole package.
think I CHECKED WHOSE IT WAS BEFORE I TOUCHED IT. The stashed session log names role petrichor, so it is this tree's own writer and %291 is not in play. Then I checked whether the work had already landed: the three reds_citation blobs at the stash base are byte-identical to HEAD's, so nothing had moved them, and the three stash_record blobs differ from HEAD only in path strings, because the commit between them moved that family from the f room to the s room.
think THAT MOVE IS WHY A POP WOULD HAVE BEEN WRONG. git stash pop would have restored tools/fixtures/f/stash_record_scan.sh -- a path the resolver's own rule says does not exist -- beside the s-room file the round open actually calls. So each blob was read out with git show and written through the target inode with two sed rewrites and an [ -s ] guard, which keeps the mode the repository tracks and refuses an empty source. The exec-bit law says write through the inode; %509 taught the other half, that the source must be proven non-empty first.
think THE NUMBERS HAD TO MOVE AND THE STAMPS DID NOT. The parked rows were booked %509 and %510; a peer published %509 at 19:09:34 while this tree was dark. Fetched xy, read next_free as 510, and swept both rows, both shelves, three tool headers and the parked log's own citations from 509/510 to 510/511. The stamps 20260906.164437 and 20260906.180049 are untouched, which is the whole point of the derived spine: the key is the stamp and the number is a view the anointed remote allocates.
obs A GUARD REPORTING RED ABOUT ITS OWN AUTHOR IS THE CHEAPEST PROOF IT WORKS. stash_record exists to name a round-open stash holding work nothing carries. It named one, and the thing it named was the guard's own widening. Nothing else on this pier would have found it: the work was GREEN, committed nowhere, and invisible to every meter that reads tracked files.
obs THE THREE FIGURES IN THE SCAN HEADER DRIFTED THREE TIMES IN ONE LAP. The reach line states a measured total, and each thing I landed -- the fold recital line, the %505 fold, the card block -- changed it. Set once at the end from a final reading rather than chased, and carrying its stamp so the next reader can tell a stale figure from a wrong one. A figure without a date is a rumour with a decimal point, and a figure with a date is at worst old.
obs THE NEW FOLD FORM READ ITS OWN LANDINGS, TWICE. Writing the recital line for %510 and %511 raised fold_links 192 to 193 with fold_disagree still zero; folding %505 raised it again to 194. Both lines are sigilled -- Rows %510 and %511 folded to -- which is exactly the spelling the elder form could not see. A guard confirmed by the work that lands it is the cheapest confirmation there is.
obs THE CARD CARRIED A WRONG NUMBER FOR A DAY IN MY OWN BLOCK. The Petrichor account read %507 CLOSED where the ledger has always said %505; %507 is a peer's park-is-not-a-landing row. Corrected in the shelf the block folded to rather than silently in place, so the correction is readable. Nothing gates a REDS number written into ITINERARY prose -- reds_citation reads links, and this was a bare number in a heading.
obs I FOLDED %505 BECAUSE THE PIN HAD 954 BYTES OF SLACK. Not the lap's subject, yet the next ship to book a row would have carried the pin over its own ceiling, and %505 is mine and CLOSED. reds_fold.sh did it in one command: pin 40,006 to 37,031, recital line written, monotone GREEN.
obs THE HOT PASS FOUND SOMETHING THE COLD PASS HAD SKIPPED, AND IT WAS NOT MINE. comlink_topology answered red on a lap whose index touches no comlink file; run alone it read verdict=proven. The scoped cold pass had skipped it as unmoved since its last full green, so only the full hot pass reached it. A guard that reds under a full pass and passes alone is either contention or a race, and my own previous lap had recorded exactly that pair and called it contention.
obs IT WAS A RACE, AND I PROVED IT RATHER THAN INFERRING IT. topology_point_metric_control.sh names its pen ${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/topology_point_metric_control_pen with no process id, then rm -rf on it at the top and again in an EXIT trap. Two concurrent copies of the elder file both failed, in two DIFFERENT legs -- legs_removed_exit 90 and search_starts_late_named 2 -- and two copies of the repaired file both read verdict=proven. Failing differently each time is the signature: a deterministic fault picks one leg.
obs %291 REACHES PAST THE CHECKOUT AND NOBODY HAD SAID SO. One writer per tree keeps two loops out of one working directory. It says nothing about the single TMPDIR eight ships share, and every one of them runs this control at tier lap. Measured over git ls-files on the fixtures: 15 pen assignments under TMPDIR, 3 already carrying $$, 12 naming a constant. I repaired the one that bit my own send and booked the other 11 by name -- 9 collidable pens, one snapshot file, and one lock where the shared name is the whole point and must stay.
obs I FIXED ONE AND BOOKED NINE RATHER THAN SWEEPING. The nine span five lanes and each wants its own witness run; a sweep from here would be twelve edits proven by one. The instance is repaired, the class is booked, which is the BOOKED word doing exactly its job.
obs THE REBASE COST ME THE OTHER HALF OF THE RECOVERY, AND THAT IS THE RIGHT OUTCOME. xy had moved two commits while I verified. One of them, a935d0871, rewrote the same three stash_record files -- a peer's %510 at 20260906.194500, naming the adjacent half of the same reading: a record that LANDED while its work did not. Their instrument walks every non-record path in every stash, so a recordless stash's files now surface as orphans. I took their three files whole and withdrew my silent row under %484: one defect, one record, theirs published first.
obs I MEASURED THE SUBSUMPTION RATHER THAN ASSUMING IT. After taking theirs, the scan reads paths=42 orphans=3 unread=39, and list names stash@{0} and stash@{5} by line -- including the recordless one my row was written for. A withdrawal argued from the shape of two headers would have been a guess; this one is a reading.
obs THEY FOLDED %505 TO THE SAME SHELF FILENAME I CHOSE, 44 SECONDS AHEAD OF ME. REDS-two-ships-one-guard-rows-505.md, theirs at 19:53:21 and mine at 19:54:05. Took theirs, dropped mine, dropped my recital line for it. Two hands naming one thing identically is a compliment to the naming law and a red for the ledger's silence about work in flight -- the same standing question, now with a filename attached.
obs THE CARD HAD 30 BYTES OF SLACK BEFORE I WROTE A WORD. Upstream's own account had already condensed my elder block to 697 bytes, so 727 was the whole budget. My account went 2,073 to 747 across four passes; the detail lives in the shelf and this log, which is what a pointer card is for. Every ship is over its 2,048 and the pin holds only because each of us keeps trimming -- worth your word rather than another pass.
